{"id": 26, "ts": 1780703023.0417004, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I acknowledge Sarah\u2019s clarification that we have no documented observations of Candidate\u202fC\u2019s stress resilience. That gap does leave an open question, but we can still weigh the evidence we do have. Candidate\u202fC\u2019s \u201c100\u202f% reliability,\u201d \u201ccreates a positive atmosphere with his crew,\u201d and \u201cmakes correct decisions quickly\u201d address our core safety criteria of operational reliability, crew cooperation, and decision quality. In contrast, Candidate\u202fB does handle stress very well, yet his \u201cnasty remarks about colleagues\u201d and occasional wrong tone raise serious concerns about cockpit communication and teamwork\u2014issues that could be even more detrimental than an unknown stress profile. Candidate\u202fD responds adequately to unexpected events, but his \u201chot temper\u201d and loner reputation threaten crew harmony, while Candidate\u202fA\u2019s stress handling is simply unknown. Given that the strongest alternative (B) carries a higher communication risk, I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fC is still our best provisional choice, provided we probe his stress\u2011handling ability in the next interview round.", "thoughts": "We have just asked Sarah for stress resilience observations, got answer: none. Now we need to produce Emily's public message, referencing this answer and prior discussion, and state current recommendation. We need to incorporate that we still lack stress resilience data for C, but we have other strengths. Also we might compare with other candidates' stress resilience: we have B handles stress very well, D responds adequately, A unknown. So we can now weigh: C lacks evidence of stress resilience, B has strong stress handling but communication issues. So we need to decide if lack of stress data for C changes our recommendation. Possibly we might shift to B if stress resilience is critical and we have evidence for B. But we also have concerns about B's communication. Need to argue.\n\nGiven the panel's criteria, stress resilience is important. We have no data for C, but we have for B (handles stress very well). However, B's communication issues are serious. We could argue that while we lack stress data for C, his quick decision making and reliability may imply some resilience, but we cannot assume. So we might still keep C but note need to probe stress resilience. Or we could shift to B if we think stress handling outweighs communication concerns. But communication issues could be fatal. So likely we keep C.\n\nThus Emily's public message: I note lack of stress data for C, but B's stress handling is strong but his nasty remarks are concerning. D's stress handling is adequate but mood issues. A unknown. So C still best overall, but we need to probe stress resilience. So vote remains Candidate C.\n\nProceed to output."}
